[FINAL-TEXT] === DIAGNOSIS ===
is_anomaly: true
faulty_devices: pc1
diagnosis_text:
The network is healthy at the hardware level; aswa2 is not dropping frames. Interface statistics on aswa2 (ip -s link, ethtool) show zero errors/drops and stable 10G/full links on eth0/eth1/eth2. Both uplinks (eth1 to dswa1 and eth2 to dswa2) have consistent VLANs, and STP is operating as designed with eth1 forwarding and eth2 blocking to prevent loops.

However, aswa2 is experiencing repeated STP topology changes driven by its access port eth0 (the PC1 port). The kernel logs on aswa2 show frequent “topology change detected, sending tcn bpdu” and, critically, “br0: port 1(eth0) received tcn bpdu,” indicating BPDUs are arriving from the downstream segment on an access port. This causes frequent MAC table flushes and ARP relearning, which present to VLAN 10 hosts as intermittent default-gateway reachability and occasional high latency to 10.1.0.1. VLAN 40 and 99 are unaffected because they are not on this access segment.

Localization: aswa2 port eth0 toward CD link_a2_pc; downstream device is pc1. Root cause: a downstream bridge (pc1 or an unmanaged switch between pc1 and aswa2) is sending BPDUs into the access port, creating STP churn, not an aswa2 hardware failure. Corrective fix: remove/disable the bridging device on pc1’s segment or connect pc1 directly; enable BPDU Guard/edge (portfast) on all access ports (eth0) and restrict the port to VLAN 10 only to prevent future TCN propagation.
=== END ===